t/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1+9{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/state.json" "file_sha256" "194be78e143009cec8748b9e74a9261c7ce81f1eebffce1410fdb84dfa3be862" "basis" 1302 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1+9{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=9{e}^{4x}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 3{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 3\\int {e}^{2x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2x}+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/state.json" "file_sha256" "194be78e143009cec8748b9e74a9261c7ce81f1eebffce1410fdb84dfa3be862" "basis" 1302 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-6002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Aca