\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{2-{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/example-7268.json" "file_sha256" "034b1652fbb8204927408d88692e8ee063626c2ce206b060635918fd80a078df" "basis" 1302 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of  the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/assets/4ed8b94a4fff3c22697264e467f955f2e51a62618d03295c4e4f38493bff2ced.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/example-560.json" "file_sha256" "2055e14597d9928b4450fb11fb41dff69c6e7dad9413a502f374072bc1208025" "basis" 1302 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= x^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =   \\dfrac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 }\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+9x}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(4+9x)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}\\sqrt{4+9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 } = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution $u= 4+9x.$ Then, $\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{9}\\textrm{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ u & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{16}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{\\int }_{4}^{16}{u}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([(16)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]-[(4)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([64]-[8]) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/example-560.json" "file_sha256" "2055e14597d9928b4450fb11fb41dff69c6e7dad9413a502f374072bc1208025" "basis" 1302 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\displaystyle  \\int_{5}^{6} } \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve? \n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}$\n\n- $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5$\n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{x}{x^2-16} + 1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/example-561.json" "file_sha256" "946a15820022627309512b7650643ac5eab28a02f221c38e68cd24904da31930" "basis" 1302 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL=\\int_{5}^{6}  \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[f'(x)]^2=\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(x) = \\pm \\dfrac {x}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/example-561.json" "file_sha256" "946a15820022627309512b7650643ac5eab28a02f221c38e68cd24904da31930" "basis" 1302 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122393" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c60ddca1af6b4e8527d4e7fd6ab2b8eea9f71c9ff4041b0311bf57f30432705" "basis" 1681 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122393" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}={sin}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c60ddca1af6b4e8527d4e7fd6ab2b8eea9f71c9ff4041b0311bf57f30432705" "basis" 1681 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122393" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16f1f4a5a74a783d73cf0a5469e5348e3c2b73357ea5d46657744a35fc26c68d" "basis" 1681 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122393" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c60ddca1af6b4e8527d4e7fd6ab2b8eea9f71c9ff4041b0311bf57f30432705" "basis" 1681 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122393" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c60ddca1af6b4e8527d4e7fd6ab2b8eea9f71c9ff4041b0311bf57f30432705" "basis" 1681 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122393" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\pi "] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c60ddca1af6b4e8527d4e7fd6ab2b8eea9f71c9ff4041b0311bf57f30432705" "basis" 1681 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122393" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c60ddca1af6b4e8527d4e7fd6ab2b8eea9f71c9ff4041b0311bf57f30432705" "basis" 1681 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122393" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c60ddca1af6b4e8527d4e7fd6ab2b8eea9f71c9ff4041b0311bf57f30432705" "basis" 1681 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122393" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={cos}^{2}⁡x+1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/state.json" "file_sha256" "8c60ddca1af6b4e8527d4e7fd6ab2b8eea9f71c9ff4041b0311bf57f30432705" "basis" 1681 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045363/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,4].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/state.json" "file_sha256" "72c85f1434e4aded17f1eff6f04d4be43b1d26f5f2bed8a329abbb7f5c40199e" "basis" 2805 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2})|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+\\frac{1}{8}(4)^{2}]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/state.json" "file_sha256" "72c85f1434e4aded17f1eff6f04d4be43b1d26f5f2bed8a329abbb7f5c40199e" "basis" 2805 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ed73d23018f9c806ed118098310e29dda00b162774569f693186c676609b18e" "basis" 2805 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/state.json" "file_sha256" "72c85f1434e4aded17f1eff6f04d4be43b1d26f5f2bed8a329abbb7f5c40199e" "basis" 2805 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{14}{29}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/state.json" "file_sha256" "72c85f1434e4aded17f1eff6f04d4be43b1d26f5f2bed8a329abbb7f5c40199e" "basis" 2805 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/state.json" "file_sha256" "72c85f1434e4aded17f1eff6f04d4be43b1d26f5f2bed8a329abbb7f5c40199e" "basis" 2805 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{4}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/state.json" "file_sha256" "72c85f1434e4aded17f1eff6f04d4be43b1d26f5f2bed8a329abbb7f5c40199e" "basis" 2805 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/state.json" "file_sha256" "72c85f1434e4aded17f1eff6f04d4be43b1d26f5f2bed8a329abbb7f5c40199e" "basis" 2805 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/state.json" "file_sha256" "72c85f1434e4aded17f1eff6f04d4be43b1d26f5f2bed8a329abbb7f5c40199e" "basis" 2805 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-5998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/state.json" "file_sha256" "72c85f1434e4aded17f1eff6f04d4be43b1d26f5f2bed8a329abbb7f5c40199e" "basis" 2805 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077006/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-15405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/assets/f74f28bf0b5bf2208b3cebf837c667cf9a9ae737d7eb758708a98548ffca8e3a.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=4{sin}^{2}⁡x$ on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-15405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=4{sin}^{2}⁡x,$ $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-15405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c6fd72147da7c67a05adf07e0dbe952abab3359d696850f85384c39670e3b00c"} {"question" "q-15405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-15405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{cos}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+4{cos}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-15405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-15315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=2\\sqrt{x}$ on $[0,1]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-15315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x},$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{x}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-15315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c6fd72147da7c67a05adf07e0dbe952abab3359d696850f85384c39670e3b00c"} {"question" "q-15315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-15315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+2x}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-15315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-6001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of the curve $y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ on $[1,e]$ is given by" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-6001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=e.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{1+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-6001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c6fd72147da7c67a05adf07e0dbe952abab3359d696850f85384c39670e3b00c"} {"question" "q-6001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-6001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{2{ln}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{2{ln}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-6001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-6000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ on $[1,9]$ is given by" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-6000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=1$ and $x=9$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x},$ $a=1,$ and $b=9.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{9x\\sqrt[3]{x}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-6000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c6fd72147da7c67a05adf07e0dbe952abab3359d696850f85384c39670e3b00c"} {"question" "q-6000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-6000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+9x\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{9x\\sqrt[3]{x}}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{\\frac{3\\sqrt[3]{x}}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-6000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{9x\\sqrt[3]{x}}}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-51270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/assets/87dc32f8909ac1c05adc981b4133a3f6e721918e4aca6923b08a073d2fb8cfe9.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-51270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/state.json" "file_sha256" "3232fbdc48098d4fbd9f2eb1854cfe68d8a0063c94bdec38c733b04d5400809f"} {"question" "q-51270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c6fd72147da7c67a05adf07e0dbe952abab3359d696850f85384c39670e3b00c"} {"question" "q-51270" "field" "selectio